content.text:
All three images are 1492×1724. Now let me check what the Webflow HTML shows — it has `width="1492"` with no `height`, meaning the live site renders these images at their natural 1:1.155 ratio. The TSX was wrong with `imageHeight: 840`.

But wait — the task says "+211/+190/+115/+548" meaning local is TALLER than live. If the natural height would be taller, then the live site must have some constraint. Let me re-read the Webflow CSS for `.full-width-image`:

The Webflow CSS at base (line 13651-13654):
```
.full-width-image {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 3768px;
}
```

No height constraint. But the card itself (`.feature-card.space-between.blue`) has `justify-content: space-between`. If the card doesn't have a constrained height, the image would render at natural ratio. But the card IS taller locally.

Wait - looking at the first Webflow CSS definition (line 2425):
```
.full-width-image {
  grid-column-gap: 12px;
  grid-row-gap: 12px;
